    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d617">

                <pb facs="0634" n="622" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg622"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.6111/1015: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-11T17:00:00+03:00">June 11, 1941—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1126. Department’s 777, June 10, 2 p.m.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d617-f1"/> While it
                    is, of course, possible that the Soviet Government may resort to reprisals, it
                    is by no means certain that it will do so in view of existing international
                    conditions.</p>
                <p>Inasmuch as great importance is attached to prestige by the Soviet Government, I
                    am of the opinion that any publicity concerning the recall of these two Soviet
                    Attachés which appeared to emanate from authorized quarters in the United States
                    might result in reprisals, whereas, should there be no publicity or merely
                    unauthorized rumors, even though they may appear in print, the Soviet
                    authorities would be less likely to resort to reprisals.<anchor n="62"
                        xml:id="d617-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d617-f1" n="61" xml:id="d617-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; but see the preceding footnote.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d617-f2" n="62" xml:id="d617-f2-content">
                <p>The Ambassador was informed in telegram No. 798, June 14, 1941, that the
                    Department did not intend to issue any statement unless compelled to do so “in
                    order to offset rumors emanating from Soviet circles or elsewhere.”
                    (701.6111/1018)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
